== Foreword
<foreword>
Iron occupies a more complex position in classical Indian metallurgy
than either of the noble metals covered in previous volumes. It is the
most abundant useful metal, the most structurally significant for
civilization, the most chemically reactive among classical dhatus, and
the metal whose graha correspondence diverges most distinctively from
Western conventions.

In Western Hermetic alchemy, iron corresponds to #strong[Mars] — the
war-metal, blood-metal, weapon-metal. In classical Indian jyotisha, iron
corresponds to #strong[Shani] (Saturn) — the metal of structure,
density, durability, and time. This is not a minor terminological
difference; it reflects different observational priorities. Western
alchemy classified iron by its #emph[use] (war and weapons); Indian
jyotisha classified iron by its #emph[physical properties] (density,
structural function, persistence). The classical Indian convention may
track the actual physics more closely: iron’s role in maintaining
structural integrity over time, its role in geomagnetic field generation
(Earth’s iron core), and its phase-transition behavior under varying
conditions all align more naturally with Saturn-quality (structure,
time, gravity, density) than Mars-quality (heat, conflict, sudden
action).

This volume documents iron’s place in the tradition with attention to
two distinctive features: first, the #emph[Iron Pillar of Delhi] — a
1600-year-old wrought iron column whose corrosion resistance has
fascinated metallurgists for over a century; second, the rich
pharmacological tradition of #emph[lauha-bhasma] used in anemia
treatment, which modern peer-reviewed research has characterized
extensively. Iron’s tradition shows both the most ancient continuous
metallurgical practice (the Iron Pillar represents Gupta-period Indian
metallurgical mastery) and one of the most empirically validated bhasma
applications (lauha-bhasma for iron-deficiency anemia is supported by
multiple modern clinical studies).

