== 1. Identity
<identity>
=== 1.1 Substance
<substance>
Iron is the chemical element with symbol Fe (from Latin #emph[ferrum])
and atomic number 26. Among the dhatus, iron is unique:

- #strong[Most abundant]: 4th most abundant element in Earth’s crust;
  the most economically significant metal globally
- #strong[Most reactive among useful metals]: oxidizes readily (rusts)
  in moist air; reacts with most acids; forms many compounds
- #strong[Magnetic]: ferromagnetic at room temperature (Curie point
  770°C) — the only ferromagnetic dhatu among classical metals (cobalt
  and nickel are also ferromagnetic but were not classically
  distinguished from iron)
- #strong[Phase complexity]: multiple allotropes (α-ferrite BCC,
  γ-austenite FCC, δ-ferrite BCC, ε-iron HCP at high pressure); multiple
  oxide phases (FeO, Fe₂O₃ in α/β/γ/δ forms, Fe₃O₄)
- #strong[Structural significance]: alloys with carbon to form steel;
  the basis of construction, transportation, and industrial civilization

Physical properties: - Density: 7.874 g/cm³ at 20°C - Melting point:
1538°C (highest among classical dhatus; well above puta temperatures of
600-900°C) - Color: silvery-grey metallic when pure; rust-red when
oxidized - Hardness: 4.0 Mohs (much harder than gold, silver, or copper)
- BCC (body-centered cubic) crystal structure at room temperature; FCC
above 912°C - Magnetic susceptibility: ferromagnetic at room
temperature; paramagnetic above Curie point

=== 1.2 Nomenclature
<nomenclature>
Per #emph[Rasaratna Samuccaya] and the #emph[Charaka Samhita], iron’s
Sanskrit names include:

- #strong[Lauha] (लौह) / #strong[Loha] (लोह) — most common technical
  term; literally "metal" (the base metal, when no qualifier is given)
- #strong[Ayas] (अयस्) — Vedic term for iron; older usage
- #strong[Tiksna-loha] (तीक्ष्ण-लोह) — "sharp/bright iron"; refers to
  wrought iron
- #strong[Kanta-lauha] (कान्त-लौह) — "magnetic iron"; refers to
  magnetite-derived iron
- #strong[Mundi-lauha] (मुण्डि-लौह) — "shaved iron"; iron filings/turnings
- #strong[Krishnaloha] (कृष्णलोह) — "black metal"
- #strong[Shastraka] (शास्त्रक) — referring to weapon-grade iron
- #strong[Kalayasa] (कालायस) — "black metal," older form

The nomenclature is more functional/descriptive than the noble metals
(gold and silver had cosmological/aesthetic synonyms; iron’s synonyms
describe physical properties or use).

=== 1.3 The three classical types of iron
<the-three-classical-types-of-iron>
Classical texts distinguish three primary types of iron based on source
and processing:

#strong[1. Munda Lauha] (मुण्डि लौह) — soft iron, low-quality, easily
worked but susceptible to corrosion. Modern equivalent: low-carbon mild
steel.

#strong[2. Tiksna Lauha] (तीक्ष्ण लौह) — sharp/hard iron, used for tools
and weapons. Modern equivalent: medium-carbon steel.

#strong[3. Kanta Lauha] (कान्त लौह) — magnetic iron, derived from
magnetite ore; considered superior quality. Modern equivalent:
magnetite-based wrought iron with low carbon, possibly with high
phosphorus content (analogous to Iron Pillar composition).

Within Kanta Lauha, classical texts further distinguish: -
#strong[Bhramaka] — gentle attractor (weakly magnetic) -
#strong[Chumbaka] — strong attractor (strongly magnetic) -
#strong[Karshaka] — drawing (medium attraction) - #strong[Romaka] —
hair-attracting (very strong magnetic) - #strong[Drawaka] — flowing
(referring to magnetic flux)

This five-fold subclassification of magnetic iron suggests classical
metallurgists had detailed empirical familiarity with magnetic property
variation in iron sources. Modern materials science recognizes that
magnetite (Fe₃O₄) varies in magnetic properties depending on crystal
size, defect density, and impurity content — the classical
subclassification may track real differences in magnetic ore quality.

=== 1.4 Graha correspondence
<graha-correspondence>
Iron corresponds to #strong[Shani] (Saturn) in the classical Indian
astrological framework. This correspondence is unanimous across Indian
primary sources: - #emph[Brihat Parashara Hora Shastra] (BPHS) Chapter 3
- #emph[Brihat Samhita] (Varahamihira) on planetary metallurgy -
#emph[Rasaratna Samuccaya] graha-rasa identifications - #emph[Charaka
Samhita] on therapeutic correspondences

#strong[This is one of the most distinctive features of classical Indian
metallurgy and deserves explicit attention.]

In Western Hermetic alchemy: - Iron → Mars (warlike, fiery, sudden,
blood) - Lead → Saturn (heavy, slow, melancholic, time)

In classical Indian jyotisha: - Iron → Shani (heavy, slow, structural,
time) - Lead → Shani (also; or Rahu in some traditions) - Copper →
Mangala (warlike, fiery, sudden) — #emph[this is what the West called
"iron"]

The Indian convention may track physics more closely: - Iron’s high
density and structural role match Saturn-quality - Iron’s role in
geomagnetic field generation (Earth’s iron core) matches Saturn-quality
(gravity, time, persistence) - Iron’s phase-transition behavior under
varying conditions (BCC ↔ FCC at 912°C) shows time-dependent
transformation matching Saturn - Copper’s reddish color and
fast-reaction chemistry match Mars-quality

The Western convention may track #emph[use] rather than physical
property — iron is the war-metal because it makes weapons, hence Mars.

This distinction matters for the framework: predictions for iron
operations are very different under the two conventions. The Atlas
framework uses the Indian convention exclusively. #emph[If you have come
to this volume from a Western alchemy background, your intuitions about
iron-Mars need to be replaced with iron-Shani for the framework to
produce coherent predictions.]

Within the dhatu/jeeva/moola classification: - #strong[Shani is
dhatu-graha] — the "structural/embodying" class (same as Chandra-silver,
Mangala-copper)

Friendship pattern of Shani in BPHS naisargika: - Shani is friend of:
Budha (Mercury), Shukra (Venus) - Shani is enemy of: Surya (Sun),
Chandra (Moon), Mangala (Mars) - Shani is neutral to: Guru (Jupiter),
Rahu, Ketu

The #emph[enemy of Surya, Chandra, Mangala] status is significant. Iron
operations are predicted to be unfavorable when these three grahas
dominate the timing window. This will produce specific predictions in
§4.

=== 1.5 Origins and Sources
<origins-and-sources>
Classical Indian iron sources were varied and high-quality. India was a
major iron producer in antiquity: - #strong[Wootz steel] — high-carbon
crucible steel; produced in South India and exported throughout the
ancient world (Damascus blades were forged from imported Indian wootz) -
#strong[Iron Pillar of Delhi] (~400 CE) — high-phosphorus wrought iron,
~6 tons; corrosion-resistant after 1600+ years - #strong[Sahyadri
ranges, Western Ghats] — major iron ore deposits - #strong[Singhbhum,
Jharkhand] — iron and copper deposits - #strong[Bellary, Karnataka] —
iron deposits - #strong[Various smaller smelters] — village-scale iron
production existed across the subcontinent

Indian metallurgy was technologically sophisticated; the Iron Pillar’s
corrosion resistance through deliberate phosphorus inclusion suggests
Gupta-period smiths understood metallurgical principles that Western
metallurgy didn’t formally articulate until the late 19th century.

