== 2. The Shodhana–Marana Sequence
<the-shodhanamarana-sequence>
=== 2.1 Shodhana (Purification)
<shodhana-purification>
Iron’s purification is more elaborate than gold’s or silver’s,
reflecting iron’s higher reactivity and the need to remove specific
contaminants.

#strong[Samanya Shodhana (general purification):]

Per #emph[Rasaratna Samuccaya], iron is heated to red-hot and quenched
successively in: 1. #strong[Taila (sesame oil)] — 7 times 2.
#strong[Takra (buttermilk)] — 7 times 3. #strong[Go-mutra (cow’s urine)]
— 7 times 4. #strong[Aranala (fermented kanji)] — 7 times 5.
#strong[Kulattha-kvatha (horse-gram decoction)] — 7 times

(Same five-media pattern as gold and silver, with iron requiring higher
red-hot temperature ~800-900°C)

#strong[Vishesha Shodhana (special purification):]

For iron specifically, an additional step is prescribed:

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 6)
+ #strong[Triphala-kvatha (decoction of three myrobalans)] — heat iron
  to red-hot, quench in triphala decoction multiple times until iron
  loses metallic character
]

#strong[Modern chemical interpretation:]

The five-media quenching cycle removes surface oxides and trace
contaminants similar to gold/silver. The triphala-kvatha step is
iron-specific: - Triphala contains high concentrations of tannins
(gallic acid, ellagic acid, chebulagic acid) - Tannins form insoluble
complexes with iron (the basis of iron-tannin ink chemistry, used for
centuries) - Iron-tannin complexes precipitate from solution and deposit
on the iron surface - The triphala-soaked iron then undergoes
mechano-chemical changes during marana that produce the final bhasma

This is functionally a #emph[chelation-mediated] surface modification.
The iron-tannin coating prevents simple metallic-iron behavior in
subsequent processing and facilitates conversion to oxide forms.

#strong[Reproducibility:] High. Standard procedure across licensed
Ayurvedic pharmacies.

=== 2.2 Marana (Incineration) — multiple methods
<marana-incineration-multiple-methods>
Iron-bhasma preparation has multiple documented methods, varying by
source material and tradition:

#strong[Method 1: Kanta Lauha route (magnetite-based)]

Following #emph[Rasaratna Samuccaya]: 1. Kanta lauha (magnetite ore or
magnetite-derived iron) is purified through samanya + vishesha shodhana
2. Triturated with triphala-kvatha to form paste 3. Cakes formed and
dried 4. Subjected to ~18 putas 5. Final product: predominantly Fe₃O₄
(magnetite phase) with some Fe₂O₃

#strong[Method 2: Tiksna Lauha route (wrought iron-based)]

Similar procedure with wrought iron starting material, ~20 putas. Final
product: predominantly Fe₂O₃ (hematite) with some Fe₃O₄, due to
oxidation of starting metallic iron.

#strong[Method 3: Bhanupaka-Sthalipaka route (modern protocol)]

Documented in modern peer-reviewed literature (Sharma et al.): 1.
Samanya shodhana + vishesha shodhana of iron filings 2. Bhanupaka
(sun-drying) 3. Sthalipaka (heating in frying pan) 4. Putapaka with
triphala kvatha at 650°C in electric muffle furnace, 1 hour 5. Final
product: α-Fe₂O₃ (alpha hematite) crystalline, reddish-violet color,
60-65% iron content

#strong[Modern chemical interpretation:]

The chemistry of iron bhasma differs fundamentally from gold and silver.
While noble metals can remain elemental or form simple sulfides through
processing, iron oxidizes irreversibly under the conditions of puta
heating:

#strong[Starting state]: metallic Fe (+0 oxidation state)

#strong[Through repeated heating-cooling in oxidizing atmosphere with
triphala mediation]: - Fe → FeO (wüstite, +2) → Fe₃O₄ (magnetite, mixed
+2/+3) → Fe₂O₃ (hematite, +3)

The thermodynamic preference depends on temperature and oxygen partial
pressure: - Below ~570°C in oxidizing atmosphere: Fe → Fe₃O₄ → Fe₂O₃ -
Above ~570°C: Fe → FeO → Fe₃O₄ - The puta temperature range (~600-650°C)
sits in the transition zone

#strong[Source material dependence]: - Kanta Lauha (starts as Fe₃O₄
magnetite ore): minimal additional oxidation needed; product retains
Fe₃O₄ character - Tiksna Lauha (starts as metallic Fe): requires
extensive oxidation; product typically Fe₂O₃

#strong[Modern characterization (peer-reviewed):]

