#strong[Convergent finding]: traditional lauha-bhasma is #strong[iron
oxide nanoparticles] with composition varying from magnetite (Fe₃O₄) to
hematite (Fe₂O₃) depending on source material and processing conditions.
Particle sizes typically in the 100-500 nm range with nano-scale
features visible under TEM.

#strong[Important note]: Loha-bhasma is #strong[NOT] simple rust. The
classical preparation produces specific iron oxide phases with
controlled particle sizes and surface properties, distinct from
atmospheric corrosion products. Modern characterization confirms phase
purity and crystalline structure consistent with deliberate processing.

#strong[Reproducibility:] High. Multiple modern studies confirm
consistent characterization across different producers.

=== 2.3 The traditional confirmation tests
<the-traditional-confirmation-tests>
The same tests as gold and silver: #emph[rekhapurnatva],
#emph[varitaratva], #emph[apunarbhava], #emph[niruttha],
#emph[nirdhuma], #emph[niswadu], #emph[nischandratva]. Plus
iron-specific test: - #emph[Mukta-pakatva] — when combined with melted
lead, the bhasma should NOT be reduced back to metallic iron (confirms
thorough oxidation) - #emph[Lauha-pareeksha] — bhasma should not respond
to magnet test (for some preparations; magnetic Kanta-derived bhasma may
retain partial response)

These tests effectively distinguish properly prepared lauha-bhasma from
incompletely processed iron filings or rust. Particle size and oxidation
state are both confirmed by traditional methods; modern XRD confirms the
phase purity that the traditional tests indirectly measure.

