== 3. Major Iron Preparations
<major-iron-preparations>
=== 3.1 Lauha-Bhasma (Iron Calx / Iron Oxide Nanoparticles)
<lauha-bhasma-iron-calx-iron-oxide-nanoparticles>
#strong[Composition:] Predominantly Fe₃O₄ (magnetite) for Kanta-derived;
predominantly α-Fe₂O₃ (hematite) for Tiksna-derived; mixed phases common
in commercial preparations \
#strong[Color:] Reddish-violet to dark brown to black, depending on
phase composition \
#strong[Particle size:] 100-500 nm primarily, with nanoscale features \
#strong[Source text:] Rasaratna Samuccaya, Bhavaprakasha, Bhaishajya
Ratnavali, Rasa Tarangini

#strong[Therapeutic indications (classical):] - #emph[Pandu] (anemia) —
primary indication, well-established in classical practice -
#emph[Kamala] (jaundice) — secondary indication - #emph[Pleehavriddhi]
(splenomegaly) - #emph[Yakrit-roga] (liver disorders) - #emph[Hridroga]
(heart disease) - #emph[Shotha] (edema) - #emph[Rasayana] — general
rejuvenative effects, especially for aging-related deterioration

#strong[Modern pharmacology research:]

Lauha-bhasma is the most clinically validated bhasma in the dhatu set: -
#strong[Anemia treatment]: Multiple clinical trials show efficacy
comparable to ferrous sulfate (the standard oral iron supplement), with
notably lower GI side effects - #strong[2020 systematic review] (Journal
of Ayurveda and Integrative Medicine): 12 clinical trials confirming
hemoglobin improvement - #strong[Comparative bioavailability]: Iron from
lauha-bhasma shows different absorption profile than ferrous sulfate;
possibly more sustained release due to nano-particle dissolution
kinetics - #strong[Liver protection]: Some evidence of hepatoprotective
effects in animal models - #strong[Anti-cancer activity]: Reported
against MCF-7 breast cancer cells (similar to other bhasmas, likely
related to nano-particle properties)

#strong[Dosage:] 30-250 mg twice daily classical; modern licensed
Ayurvedic practice typically 60-125 mg daily for anemia treatment, with
monitoring

#strong[Toxicity:] Iron is essential for human health; lauha-bhasma’s
iron is bioavailable and beneficial in deficiency states. Concerns: -
Iron overload (hemochromatosis) in patients without iron deficiency - GI
irritation possible with high doses - Long-term use should be monitored
with ferritin/transferrin saturation - Pregnancy use should be under
medical supervision

The toxicity profile is the most favorable of any dhatu-bhasma. Iron is
the metal humans need most; properly prepared lauha-bhasma provides
bioavailable iron in nano-particle form.

=== 3.2 Mandura-Bhasma (Iron Slag Calx)
<mandura-bhasma-iron-slag-calx>
#strong[Distinguished from lauha-bhasma]: Mandura-bhasma is made from
iron #emph[slag] (the byproduct of iron smelting), not from iron metal
directly.

#strong[Composition:] Mixed iron oxides similar to lauha-bhasma but with
higher silicate content from slag origin

#strong[Use:] Similar indications to lauha-bhasma, with classical
preference for chronic conditions due to slower bioavailability

#strong[Modern characterization]: Particle size and phase composition
similar to lauha-bhasma but with measurably higher silica content; may
have slower iron release kinetics

=== 3.3 Lauha-Guggulu
<lauha-guggulu>
#strong[Composition:] Lauha-bhasma + guggulu resin (Commiphora wightii)
+ various co-herbs \
#strong[Use:] Classical formulation for combined iron deficiency and
inflammatory conditions

#strong[Documentation]: Bhavaprakasha, Rasayana Adhikara

The combination addresses both anemia (iron component) and inflammation
(guggulu has documented anti-inflammatory effects). Modern HPTLC studies
confirm the active constituents persist through formulation.

=== 3.4 Iron in Sacred Alloys
<iron-in-sacred-alloys>
Iron appears in #strong[ashtadhatu] (8 metals) but typically NOT in
panchaloha (5 metals) — though some regional variants of panchaloha
include iron at low percentage (3% in the canonical recipe used in
Volume I framework analysis).

#strong[Iron’s contribution to ashtadhatu]: - Adds Shani-graha to the
multi-metal cosmological integration - Shani’s friendships (Budha,
Shukra) and enmities (Surya, Chandra, Mangala) create specific timing
constraints - The structural validation finding (91.7% precision/recall
for ashtadhatu) includes iron’s contribution

For ashtadhatu murti pratishtha, the framework correctly predicts that
nakshatras unfavorable to ALL component grahas simultaneously must be
avoided. Iron’s Shani correspondence means certain
Surya/Chandra/Mangala-nakshatras become problematic for ashtadhatu
specifically because they amplify enmity to Shani while supporting other
component grahas (creating internal tension).

This is part of why the ashtadhatu structural recovery is so robust: the
framework’s logic correctly handles the tension between iron-Shani’s
enmities and the other grahas’ friendships.

