== 10. Closing Notes
<closing-notes>
Iron is the most empirically validated dhatu in clinical use.
Lauha-bhasma for anemia treatment is supported by multiple modern
clinical trials showing efficacy comparable to ferrous sulfate with
reduced GI side effects. The classical Indian tradition’s understanding
that iron should be processed to oxide-nanoparticle form for medicinal
use is fully validated by modern characterization showing Fe₃O₄ or Fe₂O₃
at the 100-500 nm scale.

The Iron-Shani correspondence is the most distinctive feature of
classical Indian iron metallurgy. Western practitioners coming from
Hermetic-alchemy backgrounds need to update their iron-Mars intuition to
iron-Shani for the framework to produce coherent predictions. The Indian
convention may track physics more closely (iron as
structural/persistence metal aligns with Saturn-quality; copper as
heat/conflict metal aligns better with Mars-quality), though both
conventions have internal coherence.

The Iron Pillar of Delhi remains the most striking material-cosmology
case in Indian metallurgy — a 1600-year-old wrought iron column whose
corrosion resistance through deliberate phosphorus content suggests
Gupta-period smiths understood metallurgical principles formally
articulated in the West only in the 20th century. The framework predicts
timing windows that should be most favorable for hypothetical modern
reproduction of this craft.

Iron operations have the most restrictive timing predictions of any
single dhatu in the compendium, due to Shani’s three enmities (Surya,
Chandra, Mangala). This means iron-bhasma production has narrower
optimal windows than gold/silver/mercury — but Saturn’s own nakshatras
(Pushya, Anuradha, Uttara Bhadrapada) and Mercury/Venus-ruled nakshatras
(Aslesha, Jyeshtha, Revati, Bharani, Purva Phalguni, Purva Ashadha)
constitute robust favorable options.

